issues.evidence:
Ran it. File v-abs-7.hylo:
//! stage:typing no-std
fun need<A, B where A == B>() {}
trait P {
  type X
  fun f<T, U where T == Self, T is P, T.X == U>() { need<X, U>() }
}
Results: baseline binary (4587ce9e1a23, default depth): diags=0. impl10 at depth 10: flags off diags=0, fixsubst diags=0, fixsubst,eqrefute diags=1 ('no coercion from ((Self::P).X ~ U) ==> ...'). With eqrefute the counters show eqrefute.refuted=2. The fixsubst trace shows a genuine derivation: 'goal ((Self::P).X ~ U) <- try $t : ((T::P).X ~ U); coerce ((T::P).X ~ U) to ((Self::P).X ~ U) assuming $1: (T ~ Self); goal (T ~ Self) <- try $g'. Mechanism: TypeStore.unifiable(AssociatedType, AssociatedType) unifies the qualification types when either qualification is `.abstract` (TypeStore.swift ~1164), so a coercion between equality witnesses can require T ~ Self, which is not an equality between the sides. equalityIsRefuted counts AssociatedType as an atom and compares atoms with unifiable(_,_), which uses no coercion handler. It therefore never merges (Self::P).X with (T::P).X even though T and Self are in the same class.

reviews.summary:
The main claim of section 2 holds, and in two ways it understates the problem. fixsubst alone makes valid equality chains unusable. Chains of 5 or more links are rejected by the depth cap no matter how good the search is, because a genuine proof needs about 2 layers per link. Under the frozen binary's 2.5 GB limit, several of these valid programs crash the compiler with an out-of-memory abort rather than timing out. What the write-up says about the fix is too thin in four places:
- the equality theory the engine actually needs is congruence plus injectivity of nominal type constructors, with associated types treated as opaque atoms keyed by their conformance witness;
- the `canDeriveCoercions` pre-filter rejects congruence-derivable goals before any search runs, so it has to be replaced too;
- eqrefute is a narrow special case, not "the negative half": a single unrelated structured equality in scope turns it off;
- cheaper fixes inside the engine can remove the exponential cost but cannot remove the depth-cap cliff.

issues.evidence:
Ran trans-4 with fixsubst at cap 10:
- plain: 12.8 s, 639,692 matches;
- +index: 639,692 matches (identical);
- +absmodel: 639,692 (identical);
- +eqrefute: 639,692 (identical; its goals have open middle variables or lie in one class);
- +loopcheck: 6.3 s, 336,736;
- all performance flags together: 5.6 s, 336,428.
assoceq-4 with fixsubst,eqrefute: 27.8 s. 'Prefer given equalities' changes nothing: the trace of trans-4 shows 28 derivations, all completing at layer 10, and the penalty-0 winner is already the pure fact chain (no builtin given is used). BFS stops at the first layer that has any result, so preference only ranks results; it does not prune work. Dropping the builtin transitivity given (the winner never uses it, and coercing a fact x~y into a goal a~c, assuming x~a and y~c, is already transitivity with the middle fixed by the fact) plus memoizing closed equality subgoals would keep subgoals ground and the search polynomial. That is an untested hypothesis: building was not allowed. It still needs 2 layers per link, so the cap-10 cliff at 5 links remains.
